// =============================================================================
// yolo_axi_arbiter.sv — 4-to-1 mux onto the single AXI4 master port
//
// The socket in Top.vhd gives the accelerator exactly ONE AXI master. Four
// things want it: the sequencer (descriptor fetch), the conv engine, the
// elementwise engine, and the detect engine.
//
// Fixed priority is correct here and simpler than round-robin, because the
// sequencer serializes the engines by construction -- it never starts a second
// engine while one is running, so at most one engine requester is ever active
// at a time. The arbiter therefore never actually arbitrates between engines;
// it only muxes. Priority exists so that a design error (two engines live at
// once) degrades into a deterministic stall rather than corrupted interleaved
// bursts on the bus.
//
// Grant is LOCKED for the whole transaction. Releasing on rd_done/wr_done
// alone would be wrong: a new requester could win the port between the address
// phase and the data phase and interleave its beats into another master's
// burst. The lock is held from rd_start/wr_start to the matching done.
// =============================================================================
`timescale 1ns/1ps

